---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'About Shelldex: who edits the directory and how it works';
const description = 'Learn what qualifies as a runnable agent, how Shelldex checks the numbers, and how OpenClaw got its name.';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'AboutPage', name: 'About Shelldex', description,
  url: 'https://shelldex.com/about/', author: {'@type':'Person', name:'Dave Onkels', url:'https://onk.io'}
};
---
<Layout {title} {description} {jsonLd}>
  <div class="max-w-[72rem] mx-auto px-5 sm:px-8"><article>
    <header><div><h1>About Shelldex<span aria-hidden="true">.</span></h1>
      <p>Shelldex is an independent directory of personal AI agents. It helps you choose one by the job you want to give it, the computer it needs, and the access you would have to grant.</p></div>
      <img src="/images/faq-agent-homes.webp" width="1440" height="360" alt="An illustrated laptop, home computer and remote server show three places an assistant can run." />
    </header>
    <section><h2>What qualifies for the directory?</h2><p>You must be able to run the assistant, give it a goal and let it choose and carry out actions. OpenClaw and Hermes Agent are examples. Other entries may be forks, independently built alternatives or assistants for a particular device. Being listed alongside OpenClaw does not establish that a project uses its code.</p>
      <p>A submission needs the project’s own instructions for starting the agent and an example task. An extension, a library, a hosting plan or a dashboard on its own falls outside the directory. Readers can propose an addition or correction for editorial review. <a href="/submit/">Suggest a project</a>.</p></section>
    <section id="how-we-measure"><h2>How we measure</h2><p>A star is a GitHub bookmark. The leaderboard sorts available, non-archived projects by their public star totals at the posted check date. A project whose repository disappears can retain a historical profile, with its older date shown.</p>
      <p>Issue load divides GitHub’s combined open issues and pull requests by stars. Below 1% is low, 1% to under 5% is moderate, and 5% or above is high. The separate maintenance score combines issue load, the last repository push, release activity and measured changes in stars. Neither tells you whether an agent will do your job well.</p>
      <p><a href="/methodology/#star-history">How the star charts work</a> · <a href="/methodology/#health-score">How the score is calculated</a> · <a href="/methodology/#corrections">How to correct an entry</a></p></section>
    <section id="timeline"><h2>How OpenClaw got its name</h2><p>If an older guide mentions Clawdbot or Moltbot, these milestones explain the names. Dates and descriptions below follow the project’s and founder’s own accounts.</p>
      <ol class="timeline">
        <li><time datetime="2025-11">November 2025</time><h3>A WhatsApp assistant</h3><p>Peter Steinberger describes starting with a personal project that could take requests from his phone. The project’s history traces the early WhatsApp relay and the name Clawd. <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/lore">OpenClaw’s project history</a></p></li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-01">January 2026</time><h3>Clawdbot, Moltbot, then OpenClaw</h3><p>Steinberger’s announcement explains the move from Clawdbot to Moltbot following a trademark request, then to OpenClaw. <a href="https://openclaw.ai/blog/introducing-openclaw">Read the naming announcement</a>.</p></li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-02-14">February 14, 2026</time><h3>Independent stewardship</h3><p>Steinberger announced he was joining OpenAI and that OpenClaw would move to a foundation and remain independent and open. <a href="https://steipete.me/posts/2026/openclaw">Read his announcement</a>.</p></li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-09">September 2026</time><h3>A choice for missed reminders</h3><p>OpenClaw’s September release documented a setting that skips missed recurring jobs after downtime. It gives people who run agents on a sleeping laptop a choice about receiving stale reminders. <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/releases/2026.9.1">See the release notes</a>.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <p>New to personal agents? <a href="/faq/#first-task">Try the field guide’s first task</a>.</p>
  </article></div>
</Layout>
<style>
  article{max-width:48rem;padding:3rem 0;color:#392f28}header img{width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #ded4c6;border-radius:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0}section{margin:3rem 0;scroll-margin-top:6rem}
  h1,h2,h3{color:#1c1410}h1 span,time{color:#a93119}
  p{line-height:1.65;margin:.75rem 0;max-width:73ch}header p{font-size:1.15rem}a{color:#342b24;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.2em}a:hover{color:#aa311d}.timeline{border-left:2px solid #d4b6a3;padding-left:1.4rem;margin:1.3rem 0}.timeline li{position:relative;margin:1.4rem 0}.timeline li:before{content:'';position:absolute;left:-1.73rem;top:.43rem;width:.55rem;height:.55rem;background:#b73720;border-radius:50%}time{display:block;font:600 .8rem/1.5 monospace;margin:.3rem 0}
</style>
